{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3336","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-audit-mcp-doc-drift.md@sha256:671026886d222f376c52dc377e8a1922ffdc6e501d45635f64eca588cc0674fe"},"owner_reference":"3336","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nAudit `dh:*` skills for hand-written MCP tool documentation that duplicates and drifts from live tool schemas\n\n## Problem Statement\n\n`skills/backlog/SKILL.md` (deleted in #3329) re-explained MCP tool parameters and return shapes in\nhand-written prose that the tool's own docstring/`Field(description=...)` annotations already\nstated. That duplicate was structurally guaranteed to drift: FastMCP regenerates the live tool\nschema from code every server start, while a prose copy in a skill file changes only when someone\nremembers to edit it. This produced a \"12 tools\" heading against 28 real ones, a dead storage-format\nclaim, and a broken `fastmcp` invocation example \u2014 all silently wrong for an unknown period before\n#3329 caught it. `plugins/development-harness/README.md`'s \"Core backlog tools\" table has the\nidentical shape (filed separately as #3335). Neither of those two known cases is in scope here \u2014\nthis item audits the *other* `dh:*` skills for the same mechanism before it produces the same kind\nof silent, misleading drift elsewhere.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. Every `dh:*` skill (SKILL.md plus any bundled `references/`/`agents/` files it loads) that\n   contains prose restating an MCP tool's parameters, return fields, or behavior is identified.\n2. Each candidate restatement is checked against that tool's actual live schema (via\n   `ToolSearch(\"select:<tool>\")`) \u2014 the same verification standard #3329 used \u2014 not just judged\n   plausible from memory.\n3. Confirmed-redundant restatements (schema already states it, verbatim or equivalently) are\n   deleted from the skill.\n4. Confirmed content gaps (real, correct behavior the live schema does not cover) are relocated to\n   the tool's own docstring, not left duplicated in a skill file.\n5. Any finding whose fix is a genuine design decision or larger rewrite (not a same-day deletion or\n   one-sentence docstring addition) is filed as its own backlog item rather than done inline, per\n   the repo's Request Progression convention.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Re-auditing `dh:backlog` (already deleted, #3329) or the development-harness README's \"Core\n   backlog tools\" table (already filed as #3335) \u2014 both are out of scope, already tracked.\n2. Any unrelated skill cleanup, restructuring, or abstraction beyond the MCP-tool-restatement drift\n   pattern itself.\n3. Speculative edits to MCP tool docstrings \u2014 only write to a docstring when this audit confirms a\n   real, currently-uncovered gap.\n4. Auditing skills that never reference `plugin:dh:backlog` / `plugin:dh:sam` MCP tools \u2014 nothing to\n   drift against.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. Enumerate `dh:*` skills (under `plugins/development-harness/skills/*/SKILL.md`, including their\n   `references/*.md` and any `agents/*.md` they load) that mention an MCP tool name or describe its\n   parameters/return shape/behavior in prose.\n2. For each hit, fetch the live schema via `ToolSearch(\"select:<tool>\")` and compare.\n3. Classify each hit: REDUNDANT (schema already says it \u2014 delete), STALE (schema contradicts it \u2014\n   delete/correct), GAP (schema doesn't cover it but the claim is true and needed \u2014 relocate to the\n   tool's docstring), or OK (not actually a restatement \u2014 leave alone).\n4. Apply REDUNDANT/STALE deletions and small GAP relocations directly; file a backlog item for\n   anything larger.\n5. Report a summary: skills audited, hits found, disposition of each.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. Verify against the *live* schema every time (`ToolSearch`), matching the standard #3329 was held\n   to \u2014 a plausible-sounding claim that isn't actually checked against the schema doesn't count as\n   verified.\n2. Keep the change footprint to the drift pattern itself \u2014 no incidental rewrites of touched files.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n1. The item description (drafted during #3329's grooming) already proposes the exact method: grep\n   `dh:*` skills for MCP-tool-parameter-restating prose, verify each hit via `ToolSearch`, delete if\n   redundant, relocate genuine gaps to the tool's docstring \u2014 same destination pattern #3329's Phase\n   0a/0b used successfully. Planning should decide whether to reuse this manual read-and-compare\n   method as-is, or add a lighter-weight detection pass (e.g., regex for tool names in skill prose)\n   to narrow which of the ~59 remaining skills warrant a full read before the ToolSearch comparison.\n2. Scope boundary: `plugins/development-harness/skills/*` currently holds ~59 skills after #3329's\n   deletions. Planning should decide the cutoff for \"worth auditing\" \u2014 e.g., skip skills with zero\n   MCP-tool-name hits on a first-pass grep, per Design Question 1 \u2014 rather than manually reading\n   every skill in full.\n\n## References\n\n- #3329 \u2014 the closed investigation this item generalizes from; source of the verification method\n  (compare a skill/doc claim against `ToolSearch(\"select:mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view\")`'s\n  live schema) and three worked examples of it catching real drift.\n- #3335 \u2014 sibling item, same drift class, different destination (README table, not a skill).\n- Plan file `plan-this-out-private-tmp-claude-503-use-functional-lampson.md`, Phase 6 item 6 \u2014 origin\n  of this backlog item's text, written immediately after #3329's audit completed.\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| WHO is affected? | Any agent loading a `dh:*` skill that currently carries stale/duplicated MCP tool prose; maintainers who'd otherwise have to notice drift manually. | Item description |\n| WHAT observable outcome is expected? | Each `dh:*` skill checked; redundant/stale MCP-tool prose removed; genuine gaps moved to the tool's docstring; anything larger filed as a follow-up. | Item description + #3329 precedent |\n| WHEN does this run? | Once, now \u2014 a one-time audit pass, not a recurring trigger. | Item description (chore, not a feature with a trigger condition) |\n| WHY does this matter? | The exact mechanism that produced #3329's \"12 tools vs 28\" drift (hand-copied prose vs. a live-regenerated schema) is structural, not unique to the deleted skill \u2014 it will keep producing the same silent staleness anywhere else it's used. | Item description |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- None blocking. The two Design Questions above are planning-scope decisions (how wide/deep to run\n  the audit), not information gaps that need a human decision before planning can start.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] Discovery captures the item's intent \u2014 auto-confirmed: problem framing is fully specified by\n  the item's own description (drafted with direct knowledge of the #3329 precedent this generalizes\n  from), no unbound constraint, domain-knowledge gap, or contradictory requirement present. [AUTO]\n"}